Extraction telemetry that can be checked, and a progress bar that moves

Two things were wrong, and only one of them was cosmetic.

The line said "0.0 MB, 0 reads" throughout an extraction, because the counters it printed were
only filled in after the extraction finished - so a claim about cost could not be checked while
it was being paid. The counters are now live, and the byte/read figures come from the kernel
(/proc/self/io) as well as from the reader's own bookkeeping, so both have to agree.

The line also carries milliseconds per read and cues per second. Reading 1.3 MB in ~340 small
reads is a latency question, not a volume question: at 0.1 ms/read it is milliseconds, at 12
ms/read it is four seconds, at 50 ms/read it is sixteen - and one disk shared by four workers is
what turns the first number into the third. That is now visible per worker instead of guessed at.

Measured on a file shaped like the reported episodes (326 subtitle cue points, 5 MB clusters,
1.7 GB - built as a fixture, no library file touched):

  read calls   342-371 (kernel-verified)   ~1.1 reads per cue
  bytes read   1.3-1.4 MB                  ~4 KB per cue, 0.08% of the file
  wall time    21-37 ms local
  output       326 cues, identical to ffmpeg's extraction

So the reader is not doing extra work; embedded subtitles sit in scattered clusters and one small
read each is the floor. Time on slower storage is that floor multiplied by read latency, and the
new line states the multiplier.

Also: the extraction progress no longer stands at 5%. It follows the cue fraction (5% → 20%),
which is also why four independent workers previously looked synchronised - their cue counts
differed, but the number under them did not move. The run line now shows the running version, so
"which build is this?" is answered by the interface.

Harness: 117 checks, ALL PASS.

Version 1.1.0.29.
